  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nUL high is centered well to our east, toward TN/KY, but mid-level\ndry air and not so great thermodynamics (MLCAPE of 250-500 J/kg)\nhave greatly limited convection, keeping it to the Sacs and\nHudspeth County. This pattern looks to change little for \ntomorrow, so thunderstorms should largely be limited to the same\nareas. By Thursday, moisture will begin to increase from southwest\nto northeast, which will bring an increase in shower and\nthunderstorm chances and coverage. The GFS and Euro are in\ndisagreement with how quickly we will moisten up with the GFS \nmuch quicker and therefore showing more QPF on Thursday. Either \nway, it appears moisture will peak on Friday. NBM mean shows \nvalues peaking between 1.35-1.45\", and thus, heavy rain will be \nthe main concern with storms. \n\nThe moisture sticks around for Saturday, but the 500 mb high will\nbegin reforming over Southern NM, warming temperatures throughout\nthe column. This pattern will reduce overall instability and\ntherefore limit thunderstorm coverage. I suspect given PW values\naround 1.3\" we'll still see scattered mountain and isolated \nstorms. \n\nThe UL high remains nearly stationary to start next week while PW\nvalues drift downward thanks to mid-level drying. NBM mean PW\ndrops from around 1.35\" to around 1.25\" by next Tuesday. The best\nmoisture will remain west of the Continental Divide and that will\nbe where we see our best rain and storm chances. Regardless, we\nwill return to a hit and miss style forecast with most of us\nmissing. \n\nTemperatures look to largely hold steady throughout the period\nwith the NBM keeping highs in the mid to upper 90s in the\nlowlands, which is about 5 degrees above normal. Ultimately though\nit will depend on how much drying occurs and the strength of the\nhigh next week if we see El Paso add to its tally of 100 degree\ndays. Conversely, with the increased moisture Friday we may see\nhighs come in a bit closer to normal.",
